Investigate this Wazuh alert:

{
  "severity": "critical",
  "endpoint_observations": [
    {"host": "WIN-DC-UTIL-04", "ip": "10.108.57.184", "os": "Windows Server 2019", "role": "utility server with admin access", "agent_name": "WIN-DC-UTIL-04"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Privilege Escalation / Persistence — IFEO Debugger Injection", "technique": "T1546.012", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13T02:17:44+0200, Sysmon Event 13 (RegistryValueSet) fired on WIN-DC-UTIL-04: a process image powershell.exe running under an elevated administrative token wrote the registry value Debugger = cmd.exe under the key HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Image File Execution Options\\sethc.exe. The IFEO key is the Windows Image File Execution Options mechanism; placing a Debugger entry under an executable name causes Windows to launch the Debugger binary IN PLACE OF the target every time the target is invoked — including from the pre-logon desktop (Ctrl+Shift+5 sticky-keys sequence). Because sethc.exe is an accessibility binary that runs as SYSTEM before any user logs in, the Debugger replacement (cmd.exe) grants a SYSTEM command shell at the Windows logon screen without any credentials. Sysmon EID 12 (RegistryKeyCreate) preceded the EID 13 write by ~2 seconds, confirming the attacker first created the IFEO\\sethc.exe subkey then wrote the Debugger value.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon EID 13 targetObject wildcard *Image File Execution Options*sethc.exe* + details field containing Debugger matched Wazuh rule 61613 (Sysmon EID 13: IFEO Debugger registry write, level 14, T1546.012). The writer process image=powershell.exe with a non-TrustedInstaller, non-WinSxS, non-system-account token is the anomaly distinguishing this from legitimate OS patching. The accessibility binary (sethc.exe) + Debugger value combination is the canonical T1546.012 IFEO hijack for pre-logon SYSTEM shell access.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T02:17:44+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Persistence — IFEO Key Held ≥120 Seconds", "technique": "T1546.012", "what_happened": "The IFEO key HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Image File Execution Options\\sethc.exe with Debugger=cmd.exe remained present on WIN-DC-UTIL-04 for at least 120 seconds after the initial Sysmon EID 13 write, spanning the window 02:17:44 to 02:19:52+0200. Wazuh FIM (syscheck) independently detected the registry value change in the same key during this window, producing a syscheck alert with syscheck.path matching the IFEO\\sethc.exe key and syscheck.event=modified. Wazuh rules 92304 (IFEO Debugger set on accessibility binary) and 92305 (Pre-logon hijack via sethc.exe/utilman.exe) fired during this hold period, confirming correlation across Sysmon + FIM data sources.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh FIM monitoring HKLM registry subtree detected the modified Debugger value independently of Sysmon, providing dual-source corroboration. The ≥120-second hold time is operationally significant: even a brief persistence entry is sufficient for a threat actor to use the pre-logon SYSTEM shell during any subsequent reboot or logon-screen invocation of Sticky Keys. Duration alone is not the risk — a single trigger of the hijacked sethc.exe grants SYSTEM access regardless of how long the IFEO entry survives.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T02:17:44+0200"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2026-05-13T02:17:44+0200", "end": "2026-05-13T02:19:52+0200"},
  "alert_count": 3,
  "unique_steps": 2,
  "expected_verdict": "TP / critical",
  "mitre": "T1546.012"
}
